It's clear from the above that they have not been assigned in any way that
particularly relates price to utility. 

Here's an alternative explanation; some of the prices come straight out of
the AD&D First Edition Player's Handbook - leather, studded leather, scale, 
chain, splint, and banded mail all have their 1e prices; some prices were
assigned later with no particular regard for consistency, which is why
ring mail costs over twice as much as scale mail, is the same weight, and
offers one less point of AC. This explanation, unlike the idea that every
item price was carefully considered, is actually consistent with the
facts.

In view of that, the idea that you can infer from a 10 zorkmid difference
the Dev Team's views on MC3 versus displacement is hopelessly optimistic.

But, you know what's best of all? Armour costs changed somewhere between
3.0.0 and 3.0.10 (the "later" above, before which ring mail and plate mail
also had their AD&D 1e costs), and have not changed since. So your
hopelessly optimistic inference is about the Dev Team's assssment of MC3
versus displacement _in NetHack 3.0_. 
